We analyze a class of general differential equations that model electrostatic MEMS devices and have the form:

\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
\varphi\big(r,u'(r)\big) = \lambda\int_0^r\frac{f(s)}{g(u(s))}\,\mathrm{d}s & r\in(0,1) \\
0<u(r)<1 & r\in(0,1) \\
u(1) = 0 \tag{$P_\lambda$}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

where $\varphi$, $g$, $f$ are certain functions on $[0,1]$ and $\lambda>0$ is a parameter. Our study yields insights into the existence, regularity of solutions and estimates for a specific class of solutions, known as touchdown solutions, and their corresponding pull-in voltages. Specifically, in one particular instance, where the differential equation involves the operator $r^{-\gamma}(r^\alpha |u'|^\beta u')'$, we establish the exact asymptotic behavior of these solutions in the vicinity of the origin.
